MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 28f310e05cc019a48855581da30b411e, type: 3}
  m_Name: AdoboHistory
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lines:
  - text: Rooted in pre colonial Austronesian survival techniques where,
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: families preserved food in acid before refrigeration existed,
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: this dish carries a thousand year old heritage that was,
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: later documented and named during early Spanish contact.
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: Will your careful hand honor this deep tradition?
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: "Did you know the word \u201CAdobo\u201D is Spanish,"
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: but the cooking method is not. When Spanish friars arrived in the Philippines
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: ',they used a familiar word for a technique that was already here.'
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: "The 1613 \u201CVocabulario de la lengua tagala\u201D even recorded,"
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: "it as \u201CAdobo de los Naturales\u201D, meaning the adobo of the native
      people."
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: 'What came next was gradual elaboration. '
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: "Chinese merchants brought soy sauce \u201CToyo\u201D, "
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: which many families now consider essential but which was not part of the
      oldest recipe.
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
  - text: Now that's all done, Let's get to cooking!
    characterSprite: {fileID: 0}
